from mk import mk
mk("HXKz89TIlJI","Logic Pro Advanced Tutorial : Morphing with Alchemy",
 "four-source-morph-xy-crossfade-vs-true-parameter-morph-analysed-sources-per-element-additive-spectral-pitch-formant-envelope-lanes-separate-lfos",
 "Four-source morph engine: an XY/linear crossfade (centre = all four mixed, corners = one source) versus true parameter morphing of analysed (additive + spectral) sources, where the centre is a composite sound, with separate morph positions per element (additive, spectral, pitch, formant, analysed envelope), each driven by its own LFO type",
 "Build evolving hybrid textures from four contrasting sources and know the difference between crossfading and morphing. Crossfade mode only mixes the four sources by an XY point (centre = all four at once, a corner = that source alone) or along X only (A-B, B-C, C-D). Parameter morph needs analysed sources (additive + spectral resynthesis of imported samples, not virtual-analog saws) at a similar pitch; after time alignment, the centre point produces a composite of all four, not a mix. Morph can be split per element: additive partials, spectral processing, pitch, formant and the analysed amplitude envelope of each sample (not the AHDSR), each with its own position. Give each element a different LFO (slow smooth, very fast, stepped) with depth raised, keep a manual centre point, and add reverb. For Polyrhythm Studio: four analysed partial sets interpolated per element by independent control lanes.",
 ["Synth with a four-source XY morph of resynthesised (additive/spectral) sources (the video uses a DAW's bundled synth), or Web Audio / JS: four partial-amplitude arrays + four formant/envelope curves, bilinear interpolation by (x,y) per element, rendered with a PeriodicWave or additive OscillatorNode bank (implementation note)","Four contrasting sustained samples (original or free), e.g. a drone, a ghostly texture, an atmospheric event, a sustained vocal","LFOs (smooth, fast, stepped) and a reverb"],
 [("sources","list","Four contrasting sustained samples at similar pitch (the video picks C3 versions)",True),("mode","enum","crossfade XY | crossfade linear | morph XY | morph linear",True),("element_positions","map","XY position per element: additive, spectral, pitch, formant, envelope",False),("lfos","list","One LFO per element morph (shape, rate, depth)",False)],
 [("morph_patch","patch","Four-source morph patch with per-element LFO lanes"),("notes","text","Which element morphs give the strongest change")],
 ["Parameter morph does not work well with virtual-analog sources (they reset to saw); use analysed samples.","Each re-import or switch to linear mode recomputes time alignment; wait for it.","Turning fixed pitch off gives a smoother morph but the pitch wanders; keep auto-align on.","Locked (non-morphable) settings are shared by all four sources; only knobs with a morph indicator differ per source.","Pitch morph sounds the most drastic; results depend heavily on the samples. No numeric values were given; no factory samples or presets should be redistributed."],
 [("Init a patch, enable four sources with different waveforms and slightly different tuning, and choose XY crossfade. Move the XY point: centre plays all four, each corner plays one; try linear crossfade (X only: A-B, B-C, C-D). Optionally put a complex LFO on the XY point.","crossfade","Plain mixing between sources; no new timbre at the centre"),
  ("Re-init and import four contrasting sustained samples (drone, ghostly texture, atmospheric event, sustained vocal) as additive + spectral sources at similar pitch; wait for time alignment after each import.","analysed sources","Four analysed sources ready to morph"),
  ("Switch to morph XY with all elements linked and play a note; move between A, B, C, D and the centre.","true morph","The centre gives a composite sound that is none of the four, not a crossfade"),
  ("Keep auto-align on; try fixed pitch off for a smoother morph and back on if the pitch wanders too much.","morph options","Smoother morph or steadier pitch, by choice"),
  ("Split by element: reset all element points to centre, then move only the additive point; then spectral, pitch, formant and envelope (the samples' analysed envelopes, not the AHDSR). Per source, randomise morphable additive knobs, enable a spectral processor (e.g. metallize) on some, and shift formant settings so the sources differ.","per-element morph","Each element changes a different aspect of the sound"),
  ("Try morph linear: X only between A and B, C and D, or across all four.","linear morph","A one-dimensional morph path"),
  ("Return to morph XY and assign a different LFO to each element point: slow smooth on additive, very fast on spectral, stepped on formant, smooth on another; raise each depth. Keep moving the centre manually. Add reverb on the whole patch.","modulate","An evolving, experimental texture with independent motion per element")],
 ["At the XY centre in crossfade mode the sound is a mix of four; in morph mode it is a single composite timbre.","Moving only the formant point changes vowel colour while pitch stays the same.","Each element LFO produces audible motion only after its depth is raised from zero.","With fixed pitch on, the played pitch stays put while additive/spectral points move."],
 ["morph","vector-synthesis","xy-pad","additive","spectral","resynthesis","formant","lfo","texture","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="DAW synth walkthrough; factory sample names used only as examples of source types, not reproduced.")

mk("uorRN6iH87U","Patch Programmability - Episode 4 - Random Gate Generators",
 "random-gate-generators-from-noise-sample-and-hold-window-comparator-and-logic-probability-skipper-free-random-burst-clock-feedback-bernoulli-two-voice",
 "Patch three random-gate tools from generic functions: noise sampled-and-held by a clock into a window comparator, ANDed with the clock, as a probability gate skipper (window size = probability); the same with S&H output fed back into the clock rate for free random bursts; and a Bernoulli gate where comparator out and not-out each AND the clock to trigger two panned voices",
 "Make random and probabilistic gates without dedicated modules. Raw noise into a window comparator gives random gates, but only at a very small window size and with no sync or range. Instead, sample-and-hold the noise on a clock and feed the held value to the comparator: keep shift (window position) high so only the top of the held voltage range passes, and use window size as the probability control (max = all pass, fully closed = none). Because the comparator only fires when the window opens, AND its output with the original clock so every clock passes at maximum. For free random bursts, feed the S&H output back into the clock's rate CV for an unsteady clock. For a Bernoulli gate, use the comparator's out and not-out, each ANDed with the clock (a VCA works as AND), to trigger two envelopes on a higher voice panned left and a lower voice panned right; size then sets the odds of left vs right and they never fire together. Modulate size with a slow LFO and add clock feedback for a generative patch. Maps to Polyrhythm Studio as per-step random gating lanes.",
 ["Modular, semi-modular or software modular with noise, sample-and-hold, a window comparator (with not-output, or logic to invert), AND logic or VCAs, a clock/cycling function generator, envelopes and VCAs, or Web Audio / JS: on each clock tick sample r=Math.random(); pass if r falls in [shift-size/2, shift+size/2]; Bernoulli: route to voice A if in window else voice B (implementation note)","Two oscillator voices tuned high and low"],
 [("clock","signal","Steady clock (or one with rate CV for the free-random variant)",True),("window_size","number","Comparator window size = probability control",True),("window_shift","number","Window position; keep high in the skipper patch",True),("mode","enum","skipper | free random | bernoulli",True)],
 [("random_gate_patch","patch","Gate skipper / random burst / Bernoulli patch"),("gates","signal","Probabilistic gate stream(s)")],
 ["Unclocked noise into a comparator works only at very small window sizes; open it slightly and gates reach audio rate.","Without the AND with the clock, some gates are dropped even at maximum probability (the comparator stays high across consecutive in-window samples, and S&H sampling is not instantaneous).","Using raw out/not-out in the Bernoulli patch can let both outputs fire on one clock; the extra AND stage fixed this in the video.","The size knob's mapping to probability is not calibrated (roughly 50/50 near the middle). Module brands are not required; functions only."],
 [("Run noise into a window comparator; trigger an envelope → VCA on a voice from the comparator output. Set window size very low and move shift.","raw noise gates","Random gates, but only usable in a narrow knob range and unsynced"),
  ("Add sample-and-hold: noise into S&H input, a cycling clock into S&H clock, S&H output into the comparator. Set shift high so only the top of the held range passes.","clocked S&H","Random gates aligned to the clock"),
  ("AND the comparator output with the original clock (second comparator, logic module, or a VCA with clock as input and comparator as CV). Use window size as the probability: max = all clocks pass, closed = none. Listen against a second voice playing the raw clock.","gate skipper","A probability gate skipper; skipped clocks are audible against the reference"),
  ("Free random variant: patch the S&H output into the clock's rate CV.","feedback","Unsteady, burst-like random gates"),
  ("Bernoulli variant: remove the feedback; take comparator out and not-out, AND each with a copy of the clock, and send them to two envelopes: one on a higher voice panned slightly left, one on a lower voice panned slightly right.","bernoulli gate","Each clock goes to exactly one of the two voices"),
  ("Sweep size: fully clockwise = only the high voice, fully counter-clockwise = only the low voice, middle = random choice. Then modulate size with a slow LFO and add clock-rate feedback for a generative patch.","probability modulation","A self-playing, two-voice call-and-response pattern")],
 ["In the skipper at maximum size every clock passes; at minimum none pass.","In the Bernoulli patch the two voices never trigger on the same clock.","With S&H → clock-rate feedback the gate intervals are uneven.","Window size in the middle gives a roughly even split between the two voices."],
 ["random","gates","probability","bernoulli","sample-and-hold","comparator","logic","modular","generative","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Modular patching tutorial; channel CTA dropped; module names kept only as functions.")

mk("SZKIODnJuwU","This is Sample & Hold | Cubase Modulators Explained",
 "sample-and-hold-as-processor-on-lfo-or-step-input-automate-lfo-rate-up-and-s-h-rate-for-noise-filter-riser-aliasing-slowdown-random-hat-volume-inverse-fx-mix-layer",
 "Use sample-and-hold as a processor on another modulator (not as a random source): step a sine LFO at a rate, automate the LFO rate and the S&H rate separately on a filtered-noise riser so the end seems to slow down through aliasing, and turn a regular step pattern into random hat-loop volume dips with the inverse amount on an FX mix, on a duplicated layer",
 "Delta beyond our random S&H LFO skill: here S&H samples an input modulator at its own rate. With a sine LFO (unipolar, full amount) into the S&H input, a low S&H rate gives coarse steps over the sine and a high rate follows it finely, so you keep the LFO's regularity with random-feeling steps. On white noise through a filter, map S&H → cutoff at a reduced amount; instead of a linear ramp LFO riser over four bars, put the LFO rate and the S&H rate on automation lanes, ramp the LFO rate to maximum and start the S&H rate low (individual steps audible), so near the end the sampled pattern sounds like it is slowing down. For hat loops, feed a random-drawn 4-bar step modulator (1/8 rather than 1/16) into the S&H and map S&H → volume (unipolar, ~80%) for random level dips, map the same S&H inversely to a metallic FX mix, and keep the processed loop as an extra layer over the original.",
 ["DAW or synth where a sample-and-hold modulator can take another modulator as input, or Web Audio / JS: a step clock at rate r_sh that latches the current value of an LFO or step array and writes it with setValueAtTime to a gain or BiquadFilter frequency (implementation note)","White noise source with a filter","Hat loop, a volume/gain stage and an FX with a mix control (metallic/comb-like FX in the video)"],
 [("input_mod","enum","sine LFO | step modulator",True),("sh_rate","number","S&H rate in Hz or tempo division (start low for audible steps)",True),("lfo_rate","number","Input LFO rate; ramped to maximum in the riser",False),("depth","number","Modulation amount (reduced on cutoff, ~80% on volume)",True)],
 [("riser","audio","Filtered-noise riser with stepped, decelerating-feeling motion"),("hat_variation_layer","audio","Hat loop layer with random volume dips and inverse FX")],
 ["An S&H with no input modulator does nothing; it must sample something.","Set the input modulator unipolar and to maximum amount so the S&H input spans 0–100%.","The slowdown at the end is a by-product (the fast LFO sampled at a lower S&H rate); it is not a tempo change.","A 1/16 step modulator was too fast for the hat variation; 1/8 worked. Values other than the ~80% volume depth and the 4-bar pattern were set by ear."],
 [("Add a sine LFO and a sample-and-hold modulator, both in Hz. Route LFO → S&H input, LFO unipolar at maximum amount. Sweep the S&H rate from about 1 upward.","S&H on LFO","Large steps over the sine at low rates, fine steps at high rates"),
  ("Generate white noise through a filter and route S&H → cutoff; reduce the amount until it is not excessive.","noise + filter","Stepped random-ish filter movement on noise"),
  ("Compare with a simple riser: an upward-ramp LFO over 4 bars on both rates. Note it is linear and not editable; remove it.","linear riser","A predictable riser (reference only)"),
  ("Expose the LFO rate and S&H rate as automation lanes. Ramp the LFO rate up to maximum over the riser; start the S&H rate low and shape it with its own curve and a few random moves.","automated riser","A riser with irregular steps that appears to slow down at the end"),
  ("For a hat loop: draw a random 4-bar step pattern on a step modulator at 1/8, route it to the S&H input (unipolar, max), and route S&H → loop volume (unipolar, ~80%).","hat variation","Random level dips on otherwise steady hats"),
  ("Route the same S&H to the mix of a metallic FX with inverted amount, so the FX closes where the volume is high.","inverse FX","FX colour appears in the opposite places to the volume"),
  ("Duplicate the channel: keep the original loop and use the modulated version as a second layer; let other loops fill the quiet sections.","layer","Added depth and variation without new samples")],
 ["With the S&H rate below the LFO rate, the modulation shows flat steps rather than a smooth sine.","In the riser, the last bars sound slower or irregular even though the LFO rate is at maximum.","On the hat layer, loud steps have less FX and quiet steps more FX.","The original and modulated hat layers play together without timing changes."],
 ["sample-and-hold","lfo","riser","noise","automation","modulation","hats","variation","fx","web-audio"],
 note="DAW modulator walkthrough; channel intro and comments CTA dropped.")

mk("X_zozWSx-RU","Magnolia Polymove Explained: The Ultimate Randomization Tool",
 "per-voice-per-parameter-note-on-random-values-bipolar-offset-compensation-unison-chord-variation-envelope-times-morph-two-cloned-parts-per-voice-lfo-rate-aftertouch-depth",
 "Per-voice, per-parameter random value drawn at each note-on (many synced sample-and-holds): offset compensation for bipolar values at a parameter's minimum or maximum, unison/chord voices that each get different timbre, random envelope times on an arpeggiated FM pluck, per-voice morph between two cloned-then-edited parts, and per-voice random LFO rate with depth brought in by aftertouch",
 "Make every note and every voice differ slightly without sequencing anything. A per-voice random source picks a new value at each key press for each voice the key allocates and for each parameter it modulates (so 8 unison voices get 8 different wavefolder and FM values, and each note in a chord differs). Values are bipolar: if a parameter sits at its minimum, only positive values have an effect, and an open filter only moves when values are negative, so set the base knob to the middle (offset) to use the full range. Good targets: wavefolder, through-zero FM, filter, envelope times on a plucky FM arpeggio (2-octave up/down), filter envelope, and morph between two parts (clone part A to B, edit B, set morph halfway). A global effect at the end of the chain (e.g. ping-pong delay mix or time) cannot vary per voice and takes the highest value. Randomise LFO rate per voice with LFO depth at zero and aftertouch adding depth for expressive control. Maps to Polyrhythm Studio as a per-voice random draw at note-on.",
 ["Polyphonic synth with a per-voice note-on random modulator (or 'per-note random' source), or Web Audio / JS: at each noteOn create voiceRand[param]=Math.random()*2-1 per voice and apply base+depth*voiceRand to that voice's AudioParams (implementation note)","Unison or chord mode, arpeggiator, two-part morph (optional), ping-pong delay"],
 [("targets","list","Per-voice parameters to randomise (wavefolder, FM amount, cutoff, env times, morph, LFO rate)",True),("depth","map","Random depth per target",True),("base_offset","map","Base value per target (mid-range for full bipolar effect)",True),("voices","integer","Unison voices or chord size",False)],
 [("per_voice_random_patch","patch","Patch where every voice/note gets its own random values"),("notes","text","Which targets are per-voice and which are global")],
 ["Bipolar random on a parameter at 0 is half-wasted; compensate with a mid offset.","Effects after the voice mix (delay, reverb) are global, so per-voice values collapse into one.","Too many random targets at high depth makes a patch incoherent; the video adds targets one at a time.","No numeric depths were given; values were set by ear. Product-specific panels mapped to generic per-voice modulation."],
 [("On an init patch set the wavefolder about halfway and assign the per-voice random source to it. Play repeated notes.","first target","Each note has a different fold amount, both above and below the base"),
  ("Add through-zero FM as a target with its base at 0; then move the base up to compensate for the bipolar range.","offset","At 0 only positive draws are heard; with an offset the full range is used"),
  ("Set unison (e.g. 8 voices, mono) or chord mode and play.","per voice","Each unison voice or chord note has different FM/fold values; every chord sounds different"),
  ("Add the filter cutoff as a target; with the filter fully open only negative draws act, so lower the base.","filter","Per-note brightness variation"),
  ("Build a plucky FM patch with an up/down arp over two octaves at a lower tempo; randomise envelope times, then a little wavefolder, filter, resonance and filter-envelope amount.","pluck arp","An arp where every note's length and tone differs"),
  ("Add a tempo-synced ping-pong delay and randomise its mix or time.","global FX","Delay varies per note but not per voice; time changes give artifacts"),
  ("Clone part A to part B, change several parameters on B, set morph halfway, and randomise the morph.","per-voice morph","Each voice lands at a different point between the two parts"),
  ("Set a unipolar saw LFO on the wavefolder or FM amount, randomise its rate per voice, set LFO depth to zero and map aftertouch to depth.","expressive LFO","Pressing harder brings in modulation with a different rate on every voice")],
 ["Two consecutive presses of the same key produce audibly different timbres.","In 8-voice unison, voices differ from one another (wider, less static sound).","With base at 0 on a bipolar target, about half the notes show no change.","With LFO depth at zero, no LFO motion is heard until aftertouch is applied."],
 ["random","per-voice","note-on-random","unison","chords","arpeggiator","morph","fm","wavefolder","aftertouch","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Synth feature explainer; outro dropped; product UI mapped to generic per-voice random modulation.")
